]
]

#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Examples of Biases 🧐]
#v(40pt)
#only("1-")[- Overconfidence: Overestimating one's abilities 🦸]
#v(20pt)
#only("2-")[- Loss aversion: Stronger preference for avoiding losses 📉]
#v(20pt)
#only("3-")[- Herd mentality: Following the crowd 🐑]
#only("1")[
#voiceover("One example of a bias studied in Behavioral Finance is overconfidence. This is when people overestimate their abilities or knowledge, leading to excessive risk-taking.")
]
#only("2")[
#voiceover("Another bias is loss aversion, which is the tendency for people to prefer avoiding losses more than acquiring equivalent gains. This can cause investors to hold onto losing investments for too long.")
]
#only("3")[
#voiceover("Herd mentality is another bias where people tend to follow the crowd, even if it means ignoring their own analysis or judgement.")
]
]

